WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.909 --> 00:00:01.909
Pete: Hey, it's Pete.

00:00:01.909 --> 00:00:03.000
I'm here in New York.

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:05.930
And today we're talking to Dag from Tapad.

00:00:05.930 --> 00:00:09.251
Dag's the founder and VP of engineering.

00:00:09.251 --> 00:00:10.251
Thanks for being with us, Dag.&nbsp;

00:00:10.251 --> 00:00:11.251
Dag: Thank you for coming.&nbsp;

00:00:11.251 --> 00:00:15.639
Pete: So, first of all, tell us a little bit
at a high level, what is Tapad?

00:00:15.639 --> 00:00:17.109
What do you guys do?&nbsp;

00:00:17.109 --> 00:00:25.570
Dag: So, Tapad is a cross platform, or I'd
say multi-platform advertising company.

00:00:25.570 --> 00:00:31.760
We're what's called a demand-side platform,
so our customers are the actual advertisers,

00:00:31.760 --> 00:00:34.510
and they buy advertising through us.

00:00:34.510 --> 00:00:37.320
We're in what's called the real time bidded
space.

00:00:37.320 --> 00:00:45.400
This means that we are constantly processing
a lot of potential buy opportunities for ads,

00:00:45.400 --> 00:00:50.690
and we figure out in real time if you want
to buy an ad and how much you're willing to

00:00:50.690 --> 00:00:51.690
pay for it.&nbsp;

00:00:51.690 --> 00:00:56.739
And then we figure out which ad, or which
banner, or whatever you want to place there.

00:00:56.739 --> 00:00:57.879
And we do all this in real time.

00:00:57.879 --> 00:01:02.350
So it's low latency high, throughput kind
of business.

00:01:02.350 --> 00:01:03.350
Pete: Okay.

00:01:03.350 --> 00:01:05.690
And tell me about the mobile aspect to your
business.

00:01:05.690 --> 00:01:12.950
Dag: So the mobile aspect is, there are, have
been mobile ad networks for quite some time.

00:01:12.950 --> 00:01:21.680
Which are fairly, maybe not unsophisticated,
but the mobile space has been lagging behind

00:01:21.680 --> 00:01:26.120
in terms of the progress that has been made
on the online display space so, in the past

00:01:26.120 --> 00:01:27.120
few years.

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:31.220
So, especially, on the side of a real time
bidding.&nbsp;

00:01:31.220 --> 00:01:39.230
So we're trying to bring some of the more
sophisticated use cases, or uses of advertising

00:01:39.230 --> 00:01:41.690
technology, to the mobile space.

00:01:41.690 --> 00:01:49.080
And we also allow customers to do cross-platform
targeting so you don't have to go to one provider

00:01:49.080 --> 00:01:53.110
to buy mobile advertising, and to another
one to get the online display.

00:01:53.110 --> 00:01:55.390
We're more a one-stop shop.

00:01:55.390 --> 00:02:01.370
We do interesting things based on what people
are doing on their mobiles.

00:02:01.370 --> 00:02:06.670
We can use that for targeting them on their
desktops and vice versus, which has started

00:02:06.670 --> 00:02:09.570
to resonate really well with a lot of advertisers.&nbsp;

00:02:09.570 --> 00:02:10.610
Pete: Okay.

00:02:10.610 --> 00:02:15.810
So technically this means that because you
guys are doing real time bidding, there's

00:02:15.810 --> 00:02:19.671
a high volume, low latency requirement for
your architecture.

00:02:19.671 --> 00:02:21.090
Is that correct?

00:02:21.090 --> 00:02:22.510
Dag: Yeah.

00:02:22.510 --> 00:02:28.230
So basically how it works, is that whenever
an app or mobile webpage or a traditional

00:02:28.230 --> 00:02:35.990
webpage is being served there can be multiple
ad units on the page or in that app.

00:02:35.990 --> 00:02:41.600
As the page is being rendered or as the application
loads pretty much, we have to make a decision

00:02:41.600 --> 00:02:46.420
or the whole network or the whole system has
to make a decision, which includes quite a

00:02:46.420 --> 00:02:50.190
few jumps from, let's say, a mobile website.&nbsp;

00:02:50.190 --> 00:02:54.630
They call an ad exchange, the ad exchange
calls us and a number of other vendors or

00:02:54.630 --> 00:02:59.060
demand-side platforms, and we all look at
the data that we're getting.

00:02:59.060 --> 00:03:04.820
We compare that to the campaigns we're running
and we return basically a bid, saying, OK,

00:03:04.820 --> 00:03:07.680
this impression is worth this much money to
us.&nbsp;

00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:11.591
And all this has to happen before the user
actually notices because you don't want to

00:03:11.591 --> 00:03:16.900
show a page with a lot of white blanks on
it, or on some sort of spinner, loading thing.

00:03:16.900 --> 00:03:21.360
So, the requirements are pretty, pretty strong.

00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:28.280
You have to respond within typically 60 milliseconds
and this includes network round trip time,

00:03:28.280 --> 00:03:34.650
so the total processing time is very, very
short.&nbsp;

00:03:34.650 --> 00:03:40.470
And I mean there are companies that have higher
volumes than we do in the online display space,

00:03:40.470 --> 00:03:47.140
but we are currently processing about 25,000
requests per second on our servers.&nbsp;

00:03:47.140 --> 00:03:52.270
Pete: And that's one reason I heard that you
guys actually made an interesting decision

00:03:52.270 --> 00:03:53.980
to move off the cloud.

00:03:53.980 --> 00:03:56.910
Can you talk to us a little bit about that?

00:03:56.910 --> 00:04:03.599
Dag: Yeah, I mean, we started out as a lot
of companies on Amazon Cloud, and so the EC2

00:04:03.599 --> 00:04:06.690
Cloud is excellent to get started.

00:04:06.690 --> 00:04:13.970
And makes it very cheap to start running a
service.

00:04:13.970 --> 00:04:20.630
Generally though, running on a public cloud
where you have a lot of shared infrastructure

00:04:20.630 --> 00:04:27.400
makes it hard for you to really find the hot
spots, find what's costing jitter in your

00:04:27.400 --> 00:04:28.400
performance.&nbsp;

00:04:28.400 --> 00:04:34.550
So when you're running on virtualized hardware,
you're running on shared network infrastructure,

00:04:34.550 --> 00:04:37.400
you're running on typically shared storage.

00:04:37.400 --> 00:04:42.650
And we're also, we're a JVM shop, so we also
have garbage collectors which add to the whole

00:04:42.650 --> 00:04:43.650
mix.

00:04:43.650 --> 00:04:49.370
If you have too many moving parts it makes
it really hard to pinpoint where are our main

00:04:49.370 --> 00:04:51.620
sources of performance issues.&nbsp;

00:04:51.620 --> 00:04:55.840
Are they something we control or are they
in the actual infrastructure?&nbsp;

00:04:55.840 --> 00:05:02.250
And we realized that in many cases this was
actually in the infrastructure.

00:05:02.250 --> 00:05:05.030
I'm sure you can cope with that in many ways.

00:05:05.030 --> 00:05:10.460
To us being able to respond on a timely basis,
every single time is very important.

00:05:10.460 --> 00:05:13.680
So, to us the average response time doesn't
really matter much.

00:05:13.680 --> 00:05:19.310
What matters to us is how many requests or
how many incoming requests are not handled

00:05:19.310 --> 00:05:24.440
within that very stringent time that we have
to process it.&nbsp;

00:05:24.440 --> 00:05:31.050
And if we are losing ten percent of all requests
we're having a very high latency on our ninetieth

00:05:31.050 --> 00:05:32.050
percentile.

00:05:32.050 --> 00:05:35.690
That it actually might be ten percent loss
business for us.

00:05:35.690 --> 00:05:41.650
Because we cherry pick every single request
and if we miss one out of ten, that's ten

00:05:41.650 --> 00:05:43.680
dollars, basically.&nbsp;

00:05:43.680 --> 00:05:49.150
The other factor is actually in terms of cost
we see that we can, when you start scaling

00:05:49.150 --> 00:05:54.750
up a little bit, start buying more specialized
hardware, more expensive hardware then very

00:05:54.750 --> 00:05:58.090
quickly you get a better performance ratio
with your own cloud.

00:05:58.090 --> 00:06:02.130
And we also have some hardware that you don't
even get in the cloud at all.&nbsp;

00:06:02.130 --> 00:06:06.199
Like we're using solid state discs for our
key value stores.

00:06:06.199 --> 00:06:13.710
And we also have some, on the analytic side,
we have some very, very high memory type hardware

00:06:13.710 --> 00:06:18.009
and memory, RAM is something that's often
very expensive in the clouds.&nbsp;

